1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,250
You run your feature's exact prompt twice this morning and get two different answers

2
00:00:05,250 --> 00:00:05,542
back.

3
00:00:05,542 --> 00:00:06,476
Nothing crashed.

4
00:00:06,476 --> 00:00:07,992
Nobody touched the prompt.

5
00:00:07,992 --> 00:00:12,484
And when a teammate asks why, "that's just how AI works" will not survive the

6
00:00:12,484 --> 00:00:13,593
follow-up question.

7
00:00:13,593 --> 00:00:18,376
Last lecture, I said the model predicts the single most likely next word, over and

8
00:00:18,376 --> 00:00:18,668
over.

9
00:00:18,668 --> 00:00:23,277
That was true enough to build the mental model on, and it's not quite the whole

10
00:00:23,277 --> 00:00:23,627
story.

11
00:00:23,627 --> 00:00:24,502
Time to fix it.

12
00:00:24,502 --> 00:00:29,402
At each step, the model actually computes a whole list of plausible next words, each

13
00:00:29,402 --> 00:00:32,203
with a likelihood attached, not just one winner.

14
00:00:32,203 --> 00:00:35,061
Then it draws one, weighted by those likelihoods.

15
00:00:35,061 --> 00:00:39,728
Most of the time the top candidate wins anyway, because it usually is by far the

16
00:00:39,728 --> 00:00:40,428
most likely.

17
00:00:40,428 --> 00:00:45,270
But not always, and that "not always" is precisely why the same prompt can give you

18
00:00:45,270 --> 00:00:47,837
two different answers on two different runs.

19
00:00:47,837 --> 00:00:52,563
Temperature is the dial that controls how often it draws something other than the

20
00:00:52,563 --> 00:00:53,379
top candidate.

21
00:00:53,379 --> 00:00:57,871
This isn't a footnote to last lecture, it's the answer to a question you were

22
00:00:57,871 --> 00:00:59,272
probably already asking.

23
00:00:59,272 --> 00:01:03,997
If it's really just predicting the obvious next word, why doesn't the same prompt

24
00:01:03,997 --> 00:01:05,805
come back identical every time?

25
00:01:05,805 --> 00:01:10,414
Now you know: because "obvious" is a probability, not a certainty, and the dial

26
00:01:10,414 --> 00:01:12,573
decides how strictly it sticks to it.

27
00:01:13,123 --> 00:01:17,040
Same prompt, five separate runs, asking for one product tagline.

28
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:18,203
Watch what changes.

29
00:01:18,203 --> 00:01:23,162
Near zero, it's almost always the same answer, run after run, because the dial is

30
00:01:23,162 --> 00:01:26,835
telling it to take the top candidate essentially every time.

31
00:01:26,835 --> 00:01:29,834
Reliable, a little safe, occasionally repetitive.

32
00:01:29,834 --> 00:01:34,487
Near one, the same prompt produces genuinely different taglines across runs.

33
00:01:34,487 --> 00:01:39,445
Same input, real variety, because the dial is now telling it to draw from further

34
00:01:39,445 --> 00:01:41,465
down that ranked list more often.

35
00:01:41,465 --> 00:01:46,423
Neither setting is "better." They're tuned for different jobs, and the mistake is

36
00:01:46,423 --> 00:01:50,035
picking one without asking which job you're actually doing.

37
00:01:50,035 --> 00:01:54,993
Notice, too, that even at zero, the first tagline could still have been the wrong

38
00:01:54,993 --> 00:01:56,034
one to lead with.

39
00:01:56,034 --> 00:02:00,686
Consistency tells you nothing about whether the answer it kept repeating was

40
00:02:00,686 --> 00:02:01,543
actually good.

41
00:02:02,093 --> 00:02:07,028
That's the term, and notice the definition doesn't say "creative" or "smart." It

42
00:02:07,028 --> 00:02:11,161
says how often the model picks something other than the top choice.

43
00:02:11,161 --> 00:02:11,778
That's it.

44
00:02:11,778 --> 00:02:16,589
Everything else people say about temperature is a description of what that one

45
00:02:16,589 --> 00:02:18,748
mechanical change tends to produce.

46
00:02:18,748 --> 00:02:23,682
The exercise is the fastest way to feel a setting you've probably never actually

47
00:02:23,682 --> 00:02:24,299
looked at.

48
00:02:24,299 --> 00:02:29,049
Run your feature's real prompt five times, at whatever temperature is already

49
00:02:29,049 --> 00:02:32,380
configured, and count the genuinely different answers.

50
00:02:32,380 --> 00:02:36,327
Most people are surprised by what they find, in both directions.

51
00:02:36,877 --> 00:02:39,255
Here's a rough default, and I mean rough.

52
00:02:39,255 --> 00:02:42,445
Treat it as a starting point to argue with, not a rule.

53
00:02:42,445 --> 00:02:47,142
Anything where consistency itself is the feature, summarising the same ticket the

54
00:02:47,142 --> 00:02:51,608
same way every time, extracting the same field reliably, wants a low setting.

55
00:02:51,608 --> 00:02:56,248
Code generation too: you want the same, boring, working answer, not an inventive

56
00:02:56,248 --> 00:02:56,479
one.

57
00:02:56,479 --> 00:03:01,177
Anything where variety is the actual point, brainstorming names, drafting several

58
00:03:01,177 --> 00:03:04,193
marketing angles to choose between, wants it higher.

59
00:03:04,193 --> 00:03:06,571
You're not asking for one correct answer.

60
00:03:06,571 --> 00:03:08,078
You're asking for options.

61
00:03:08,078 --> 00:03:12,950
If you don't know which one your feature is, that's worth answering before you argue

62
00:03:12,950 --> 00:03:13,936
about the number.

63
00:03:13,936 --> 00:03:18,459
And if a single feature does both, a support tool that drafts a reply and also

64
00:03:18,459 --> 00:03:23,215
suggests three subject lines, it may genuinely need two different settings for two

65
00:03:23,215 --> 00:03:26,173
different calls, not one compromise value for both.

66
00:03:26,723 --> 00:03:31,508
Say the honest caveat, because this is the misunderstanding that actually costs

67
00:03:31,508 --> 00:03:31,932
people.

68
00:03:31,932 --> 00:03:35,748
A low temperature setting does not make the model more correct.

69
00:03:35,748 --> 00:03:37,444
It makes it more consistent.

70
00:03:37,444 --> 00:03:42,411
If the most likely answer happens to be wrong, a temperature of zero will give you

71
00:03:42,411 --> 00:03:45,743
that same wrong answer, confidently, every single time.

72
00:03:45,743 --> 00:03:50,588
Consistency and correctness are two completely different properties, and turning

73
00:03:50,588 --> 00:03:52,708
this dial only touches one of them.

74
00:03:52,708 --> 00:03:57,797
Whether it's actually right is a question for the golden set and rubric you'll build

75
00:03:57,797 --> 00:03:59,917
in Section four, not for this dial.

76
00:03:59,917 --> 00:04:04,157
This dial has never been able to answer that question, at any setting.

77
00:04:04,707 --> 00:04:06,293
One thing before you move on.

78
00:04:06,293 --> 00:04:10,232
Find out what temperature your feature is actually running at right now.

79
00:04:10,232 --> 00:04:14,718
Most people building on top of these models have never checked, and it's usually a

80
00:04:14,718 --> 00:04:16,468
default nobody chose on purpose.

81
00:04:16,468 --> 00:04:21,009
Then match the setting to the job you're doing, consistency or variety, rather than

82
00:04:21,009 --> 00:04:23,635
whatever the example code happened to ship with.

83
00:04:23,635 --> 00:04:28,230
Next lecture moves from how it decides to what it's actually capable of: the honest,

84
00:04:28,230 --> 00:04:31,075
current state of what these models can and can't do.
